+/*
+ * 6.12 mainline port (SBE1V1K, stage W1).
+ *
+ * The vendor kernel extends <linux/ppp_channel.h> with a PPP channel
+ * connection notifier (ppp_channel_connection_register_notify(),
+ * PPP_CHANNEL_CONNECT/DISCONNECT) and adds helpers that reach into the
+ * pppox socket from a struct net_device:
+ *	ppp_is_multilink(), ppp_hold_channels(),
+ *	ppp_channel_get_protocol(), ppp_release_channels(),
+ *	pppoe_channel_addressing_get().
+ * Mainline 6.12 has none of them, and it exposes no other in-kernel
+ * interface that reports a PPPoE session's session-id and server MAC.
+ * The vendor manager therefore cannot be rebuilt as-is.
+ *
+ * Behavioural consequence: the session table stays empty, so
+ * sfe_pppoe_mgr_find_session() always returns false and SFE declines to
+ * accelerate PPPoE-encapsulated flows.  Those flows are still forwarded
+ * normally by the Linux stack (SFE just does not offload them), which is
+ * also what happens when a session is missing on the vendor build.
+ * Non-PPPoE flows are unaffected.  This is a documented gap of the
+ * mainline port, not a functional equivalence.
+ *
+ * The public interface (sfe_pppoe_mgr.h) is kept unchanged so that
+ * sfe.c, sfe_ipv4.c and sfe_ipv6.c need no changes of their own.
+ */
